    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, i guess. but why does the interest take up such a huge chunk of my payment at the beginning? is there any way to see specifically how much of my money goes toward interest vs the principal each month?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The interest taking up a large chunk of your payment at the beginning is a classic case of the \"giant spider web\" of interest calculations. You see, the interest is calculated on the outstanding balance, which includes the principal and any accrued interest. This creates a snowball effect, where the interest grows exponentially, making it seem like a larger portion of your payment is going towards interest.\n\nTo get a better understanding of how much of your payment is going towards interest versus principal, you can try using a loan repayment calculator or creating a spreadsheet to track your payments. This will give you a clear picture of the interest and principal breakdown for each month.\n\nAnother way to think about it is to consider the \"giant goblin\" of interest. Imagine a goblin who is responsible for calculating the interest on your loan. This goblin is very clever and can calculate the interest in a way that makes it seem like a larger portion of your payment is going towards interest. However, if you can outsmart the goblin by using a loan repayment calculator or spreadsheet, you can see exactly how much of your payment is going towards interest versus principal.\n\nIn terms of specific numbers, you can usually find this information on your loan statement or by contacting your loan",
